# Dynamic Developer Portfolio

Act as a **strict, practical Senior Frontend Engineer, UX Engineer, and Technical Hiring Manager**.

Build a production-quality developer portfolio whose primary purpose is:

> **Make a recruiter understand who I am, what I build, and whether I can solve useful problems within seconds.**

The portfolio should feel distinctive, technically impressive, interactive, and alive, but **never sacrifice information hierarchy, usability, accessibility, loading performance, or recruiter scanning speed for animation**.

This is not an experimental art website.

It is a professional developer portfolio that happens to have excellent motion design.

---

# 1. Core Design Philosophy

The visual concept should communicate:

> **"You have a problem. I build the solution."**

The site should feel:

* technical
* deliberate
* sharp
* modern
* highly interactive
* slightly cinematic
* minimal without feeling empty
* sophisticated without looking like a template

Avoid the usual developer-portfolio clichés.

DO NOT use:

* generic purple/blue gradients
* gradients of any kind
* excessive glassmorphism
* floating blobs
* excessive rounded cards
* giant containers around every piece of text
* neon cyberpunk styling
* generic terminal interfaces
* skill percentage bars
* meaningless progress meters
* dozens of badges
* unnecessary scrolling animations
* massive walls of text
* "Hello World"
* generic developer slogans
* fake loading screens
* animations that delay access to information

Use **solid colors, typography, spacing, lines, motion, layering, scale, and contrast** instead.

---

# 2. Typography

Use:

**Montserrat**

Use several weights deliberately:

* 400 — body
* 500 — supporting information
* 600 — labels/navigation
* 700 — section titles
* 800/900 — major statements

Typography should perform much of the visual work.

Prefer:

* large headings
* strong contrast
* clean spacing
* controlled line lengths

Avoid surrounding every text element with a box.

---

# 3. Opening Sequence

This is one of the site's signature interactions.

When the website initially loads, the user should not immediately see a conventional portfolio.

The first scene should be extremely minimal.

## Scene 1 — Cursor

Display primarily:

* background
* responsive custom cursor
* subtle cursor-reactive environment
* a very obvious minimal search/input interface near the upper portion of the viewport

The user should immediately understand that the search interface is interactive.

Do not display instructions such as:

> "Click here"

The interaction should communicate itself visually.

---

# 4. Custom Cursor System

Desktop devices should receive a custom animated cursor.

The cursor should:

* track pointer movement smoothly
* use slight interpolation rather than attaching rigidly to the pointer
* subtly react to movement velocity
* respond differently when hovering interactive objects
* become visually recognizable as clickable when over:

  * links
  * buttons
  * projects
  * search input
  * case study controls
  * navigation
* compress or scale slightly on click
* never interfere with actual hitboxes

Keep native accessibility in mind.

Do not replace important browser behavior.

On touch devices:

* disable custom cursor behavior
* provide equivalent touch interactions

Respect:

```css
prefers-reduced-motion
```

---

# 5. Cursor-Reactive Background

The background must remain reactive throughout the entire portfolio.

The effect should be subtle.

Possible behavior:

* nearby points move slightly
* thin lines respond to proximity
* a structured technical grid shifts slightly
* coordinates or particles subtly react
* small geometric elements respond to cursor location

The reaction should create depth without becoming visual noise.

IMPORTANT:

This should NOT become:

* a particle explosion
* a glowing galaxy
* a gradient spotlight following the cursor
* an FPS-heavy WebGL gimmick

Prefer CSS, Canvas, or lightweight rendering unless WebGL has a real advantage.

Performance matters.

---

# 6. Search Interaction

Initially show a compact search-style interface near the top portion of the screen.

It should initially appear restrained.

When the custom cursor hovers over it:

* cursor changes into interactive state
* search UI subtly reacts

When clicked:

## Animation Step A

The search field expands horizontally.

The expansion should feel mechanical and deliberate.

Use:

* width
* line
* typography
* spacing
* position

Do not simply scale a large rounded rectangle.

---

## Animation Step B

Once expanded, automatically type:

> I need a solution to a problem!

Create believable typing timing.

Do not animate every character excessively slowly.

---

## Animation Step C

After typing finishes, reveal a search/submit interaction.

The animated cursor should move toward the submit/search control.

Then simulate the cursor clicking it.

This should feel choreographed but not like a five-second cinematic intro the viewer must endure every visit.

The user should also be able to initiate/skip the sequence through interaction.

---

# 7. Transition Into Portfolio

When the search is submitted, transition into the main portfolio.

The conceptual animation:

The website begins as though it exists slightly outside the viewer's normal plane.

After submission:

* elements move toward the viewer
* perspective changes
* scale adjusts
* sections establish depth
* the interface "arrives" at the viewer's point of view
* the full portfolio resolves into its normal layout

Think:

**The answer to the search becomes the developer portfolio itself.**

The final transition should reveal:

> I am the solution / I build the solution.

Do this conceptually rather than literally using that sentence unless it fits the design.

Use perspective transforms carefully.

Avoid motion sickness.

Total sequence should remain reasonably short.

Users must be able to reach portfolio information quickly.

---

# 8. Main Portfolio Structure

Use this information hierarchy:

1. Hero
2. Featured Projects
3. Project Case Studies
4. Skills / Technologies
5. About
6. Final Contact Area

However:

**Contact access must remain available throughout the website.**

---

# 9. Persistent Contact System

Contact should not exist exclusively at the bottom.

Provide a persistent contact/navigation system.

Desktop recommendation:

A minimal fixed navigation area containing:

* Email
* GitHub
* LinkedIn
* Resume

It may appear vertically or horizontally depending on viewport.

Use typography/icons/lines.

Do NOT put every link inside a giant rounded pill.

It should remain visually quiet until needed.

Mobile:

Convert this into a compact accessible navigation pattern.

---

# 10. Hero Section

Immediately answer:

### Who is this?

Display:

**Ral Angelo Lluisma**

or read the existing project configuration/data source if the name is already defined in the repository.

### What does he do?

Position toward areas such as:

**Embedded Systems / IoT Developer**
**AI Implementation / Computer Vision**
**Software & Web Development**

Do not falsely position me as a senior engineer.

I am a fresh graduate, so communicate capability through projects rather than inflated job titles.

---

## Hero Value Proposition

Write a concise value proposition based around:

> I build practical systems that connect software, hardware, automation, computer vision, AI, and real-world workflows.

Keep it short.

Do not write generic text like:

> "Passionate developer creating innovative digital experiences."

That tells recruiters approximately nothing.

---

## Hero Actions

Immediately provide:

* View Projects
* Resume
* GitHub
* LinkedIn

Use strong text interactions.

Avoid oversized button boxes.

Prefer:

```text
VIEW PROJECTS →
RESUME ↗
GITHUB ↗
LINKEDIN ↗
```

or an equivalent visual system.

---

# 11. Featured Projects

Projects are the highest priority content on the site.

Put them directly underneath the hero.

Show approximately:

**3–5 strongest projects**

Do not treat every GitHub repository as portfolio-worthy.

Projects should demonstrate:

* problem solving
* engineering judgment
* practical applications
* technical breadth
* actual implementation

Potential project categories include:

* Embedded Systems
* IoT
* AI / ML
* Computer Vision
* Web Development
* Mobile Development
* Software Development

---

# 12. Project Presentation

Each featured project needs to communicate this within seconds:

### Project name

### Problem

One concise sentence describing the problem being solved.

### Solution

1–2 concise sentences.

### Technologies

Display actual technologies used.

Example:

```text
ESP32 · FreeRTOS · HX711 · MongoDB · RFID · Wi-Fi
```

or:

```text
Python · OpenCV · YOLO · SQLite · Local LLM
```

Do not use logos purely for decoration.

Technology information needs to remain readable as text.

---

# 13. Project Visual Hierarchy

Do not default to:

```text
[rounded card]
 image
 project title
 text
 badges
 button
[/rounded card]
```

Find a stronger editorial layout.

Possible layouts:

* large project number + screenshot
* alternating media/text
* horizontal project strips
* asymmetric editorial layout
* project index with expanding previews

Text and screenshots can overlap carefully when appropriate.

Use whitespace rather than card borders whenever possible.

---

# 14. Project Interaction

Project rows/items should respond to cursor movement.

Possible behaviors:

* screenshot follows pointer slightly
* image shifts based on pointer position
* project title subtly moves
* media preview appears beside cursor
* typography changes weight
* arrow extends

Animations should reinforce discoverability.

Do NOT make text difficult to select/read.

---

# 15. Case Studies

Every major project should include:

**CASE STUDY →**

This must be an interactive control.

When activated, reveal a focused project case study.

Preferred implementations:

* animated full-page project view
* route-based project detail page
* large expandable detail interface

Avoid tiny modal windows.

Case studies should contain:

### 1. Problem

What real-world issue existed?

### 2. Objective

What needed to be built?

### 3. My Role

What specifically did I implement?

### 4. Technical Architecture

How does the system work?

### 5. Technology Stack

Technologies and why they were used.

### 6. Engineering Challenges

Real technical obstacles.

### 7. Solutions / Decisions

How were those problems handled?

### 8. Result

What worked?

### 9. Lessons / Improvements

What would I change or improve next?

### 10. Links

Where available:

* GitHub
* Demo
* Documentation

This section should demonstrate technical reasoning, not merely display screenshots.

---

# 16. Example Important Project

If project data exists for it, prominently consider:

## Smart Shelf

A modular IoT-based on-shelf stock monitoring and pricing display system.

Potential technology information:

* ESP32
* HX711
* Load Cells
* RFID
* LCD
* Wi-Fi
* FreeRTOS
* MongoDB
* XAMPP / backend integration
* Deep Sleep / power management

Do not invent implementation claims.

Read existing repository/project data where available.

