# 17. Skills Section

Do NOT create percentage bars.

Bad:

```text
JavaScript ████████ 80%
Python ██████ 60%
```

Skill percentages are meaningless unless apparently developers have evolved into RPG characters.

Instead group technologies by purpose.

Example:

### Embedded / IoT

ESP32
Arduino
FreeRTOS
RFID
Sensors
Serial Communication
Electronics

### AI / Computer Vision

Python
OpenCV
YOLO
Local AI Models
Data Annotation

### Web

JavaScript / TypeScript
React
Next.js
HTML
CSS
Tailwind

### Data / Backend

MongoDB
SQL / SQLite
REST APIs

### Tools

Git
GitHub
Figma
VS Code
Linux

ONLY show technologies genuinely supported by project data, resume, or existing portfolio information.

Do not fabricate proficiency.

---

# 18. Skills Interaction

Skills may have subtle interactive behavior.

For example:

Hovering a technology can highlight projects that used it.

Example:

Hover:

> ESP32

Then related project titles subtly become emphasized.

This makes the technology section evidence-based rather than decorative.

---

# 19. About Section

Place the full About section toward the bottom.

Do NOT place a huge biography near the beginning.

Recruiters care about demonstrated ability first.

The About section should briefly communicate:

* Embedded Systems / IoT educational background
* interest in building practical systems
* combination of software + hardware experience
* interest in AI/computer vision
* ability to learn across domains
* current direction as an early-career developer

Keep this human and concise.

Do not turn it into an autobiography.

---

# 20. Contact Section

The bottom contact section should be a strong ending.

Possible headline:

> Have a problem worth solving?

or a similarly concise message.

Provide:

* email
* GitHub
* LinkedIn
* resume
* optional contact form

Contact form should include:

* Name
* Email
* Message

Use proper validation.

Do not require unnecessary information.

---

# 21. Navigation

Keep navigation extremely simple.

Possible structure:

```text
PROJECTS
STACK
ABOUT
CONTACT
```

Navigation should support smooth movement between sections.

Highlight current section subtly.

Do not create huge navigation panels.

---

# 22. Motion Design Rules

Motion must communicate one of these:

* hierarchy
* interaction
* navigation
* causality
* state change
* depth

If an animation communicates none of them, remove it.

Use motion for:

* opening sequence
* section entrance
* project hover
* navigation
* case study transitions
* cursor interaction
* background response

Avoid animating every paragraph simply because an animation library exists.

---

# 23. Animation Timing

General interaction timing:

Micro interactions:

```text
120–250ms
```

Section transitions:

```text
300–600ms
```

Major cinematic transition:

```text
600–1200ms
```

Use easing curves that feel responsive.

Avoid excessive spring bouncing.

---

# 24. Scrolling

Scrolling should remain mostly native.

Do not hijack the browser wheel.

Do not make users scroll several times before the page actually moves.

Use subtle scroll-linked animations where useful.

Potential examples:

* project media shifts slightly
* section numbers move
* thin navigation progress indicator

Keep scrolling predictable.

---

# 25. Visual Language

Use a restrained solid color system.

Example direction:

### Background

Near-black, charcoal, warm white, or another carefully chosen solid tone.

### Foreground

Strong contrasting typography.

### Accent

ONE strong accent color.

Optional secondary neutral tones.

NO GRADIENTS.

Use accent color intentionally for:

* interactive states
* cursor
* links
* project identifiers
* active navigation
* technical annotations

---

# 26. Lines Instead of Boxes

Where structure is necessary, prefer:

* divider lines
* whitespace
* typography
* alignment
* scale
* subtle background shifts

Instead of:

* bordered rounded containers
* card grids everywhere

Project boundaries can use something as simple as:

```text
────────────────────────────────────────────
01    SMART SHELF                  CASE STUDY →
────────────────────────────────────────────
```

with media and descriptions integrated naturally.

---

# 27. Responsive Design

The site must be fully usable at:

* 320px mobile
* standard phones
* tablets
* laptops
* desktops
* ultrawide displays

Do not simply shrink the desktop design.

Mobile should have deliberate behavior.

Disable effects that rely on hover when hover does not exist.

The opening search interaction must still work on touch.

---

# 28. Accessibility

Meet practical WCAG expectations.

Requirements:

* semantic HTML
* proper heading hierarchy
* keyboard navigation
* visible keyboard focus states
* meaningful link names
* alt text
* sufficient color contrast
* reduced-motion support
* buttons must actually be `<button>`
* navigation should use `<nav>`
* project links should be real anchors/routes
* forms require labels
* animations cannot trap interaction

Custom cursor must never make the interface inaccessible.

---

# 29. Performance

Target:

* Lighthouse Performance: 90+
* Accessibility: 95+
* Best Practices: 95+
* SEO: 90+

Keep the initial bundle reasonable.

Optimize:

* screenshots
* video
* fonts
* animations
* event listeners
* canvas effects

Use:

* lazy loading
* responsive images
* modern image formats
* code splitting where useful

Avoid constant React re-renders caused by mouse movement.

Use refs/requestAnimationFrame for high-frequency animation where appropriate.

---

# 30. SEO

Include:

* semantic title
* meta description
* OpenGraph metadata
* social preview
* favicon
* canonical metadata if applicable

Use structured content that search engines can understand.

---

# 31. Architecture

Keep the code maintainable.

Do not create a single 2,000-line page component.

Separate responsibilities.

Suggested structure:

```text
components/
  navigation/
  cursor/
  background/
  hero/
  projects/
  case-study/
  skills/
  about/
  contact/
  motion/

data/
  projects.ts
  skills.ts
  profile.ts

hooks/
  useCursor.ts
  useReducedMotion.ts
  usePointerPosition.ts

lib/
```

Adapt to the existing repository rather than blindly replacing its architecture.

---

# 32. Data-Driven Projects

Project information must live in structured data.

Example model:

```ts
interface Project {
  id: string
  title: string
  category: string
  summary: string
  problem: string
  solution: string
  technologies: string[]
  images: string[]
  github?: string
  demo?: string
  featured: boolean
  caseStudy: {
    objective: string
    role: string
    architecture: string
    challenges: string[]
    solutions: string[]
    outcome: string
    lessons: string[]
  }
}
```

Rendering should consume this data.

Do not manually duplicate project information throughout the UI.

---

# 33. Progressive Enhancement

Core content must remain understandable even if animation fails.

Projects should not disappear because JavaScript failed to calculate an animation state.

The website is an information system first.

Animation is enhancement.

---

# 34. Recruiter Test

Continuously evaluate the page using these tests.

## Five-Second Test

Within five seconds after reaching the main portfolio, can a recruiter answer:

1. Who is this?
2. What kind of developer is he?
3. What does he build?
4. Where are his strongest projects?
5. How do I contact him?

If not, simplify.

---

## Thirty-Second Test

Within thirty seconds, can the recruiter identify:

* strongest project
* technology experience
* technical specialty
* educational/early-career status
* GitHub
* resume
* contact method

If not, improve information hierarchy.

---

# 35. Senior Developer Review Rule

Whenever forced to choose between:

> "That animation looks cool."

and

> "The information is easier to understand."

Choose information clarity.

Whenever both are possible, achieve both.

---

# 36. Implementation Behavior

Before modifying code:

1. Inspect the existing repository.
2. Understand its framework.
3. Locate existing:

   * components
   * project data
   * assets
   * styles
   * fonts
   * routes
4. Preserve useful existing functionality.
5. Do not rewrite working systems unnecessarily.

Then implement incrementally.

---

# 37. Technical Stack

If the repository already has an established stack, continue using it.

If starting from scratch, prefer:

* Next.js
* TypeScript
* React
* Tailwind CSS or carefully structured CSS
* Framer Motion / Motion
* Lucide icons where necessary

Do not introduce several overlapping animation frameworks.

One animation system is enough.

For pointer animation, requestAnimationFrame and CSS transforms may be preferable to storing pointer positions in React state.

---

# 38. Development Priorities

Implement in this order:

### Phase 1

Core structure and responsive information architecture.

### Phase 2

Project system and case studies.

### Phase 3

Navigation and persistent contact.

### Phase 4

Opening interaction.

### Phase 5

Custom cursor and reactive background.

### Phase 6

Motion refinement.

### Phase 7

Accessibility and reduced-motion handling.

### Phase 8

Performance optimization.

### Phase 9

Final visual polish.

Never build the cinematic intro before the actual portfolio works.

---

# 39. Avoid Fake Content

Do not fabricate:

* employment
* clients
* testimonials
* performance metrics
* project users
* technical achievements
* skills
* production deployments

If information is unavailable:

create a clear TODO or placeholder in the data model.

Do not write fictional accomplishments simply to make the portfolio look impressive.

---

# 40. Final Quality Standard

The final site should feel like:

**A technically minded developer designed an elegant interactive system around his own work.**

Not:

**A frontend animation showcase desperately trying to become a résumé.**

The portfolio must demonstrate:

* engineering thinking
* interaction design
* visual discipline
* attention to detail
* performance awareness
* practical problem solving

Animations should make visitors curious.

Projects should make them stay.

Technical reasoning should make them consider hiring me.

Contact information should make reaching me effortless.

---

# Definition of Done

Do not consider implementation complete until:

* Opening search interaction works
* Animated typing works
* Search-to-portfolio perspective transition works
* Cursor interaction works on desktop
* Touch fallback works
* Background remains cursor-reactive
* No gradients are present
* Montserrat is implemented correctly
* Hero communicates role/value immediately
* Featured projects appear directly after hero
* Each project lists technologies
* Each major project has a functional Case Study interaction
* Case studies explain engineering decisions
* Skills are grouped without percentages
* About appears toward the bottom
* Contact links remain accessible throughout the site
* Bottom contact section exists
* Mobile layout is deliberately designed
* Keyboard navigation works
* `prefers-reduced-motion` works
* project content is data-driven
* animations do not significantly harm performance
* no fake information was introduced
* no major console errors exist
* production build succeeds

Finally, inspect the entire finished website as a **Senior Developer reviewing a fresh graduate for hire**.

Remove anything that:

* wastes time
* obscures technical ability
* looks generic
* exists purely for decoration
* makes important information harder to find

Strengthen anything that demonstrates actual engineering judgment.
